  "live_records": {
    "q-47536": {
      "dom_id": "step-q47536",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205298/assets/4b280f9756119d26317899e07e9a23b3679555701b23096516afb7a646cda453.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=5$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=5$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{f(5+1)-f(5-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(6)-f(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
