    "q-344028": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344028",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14205298/assets/0bfc66c5e19ff0fe88cba465c6b045402fc5f8753befa4acb3b1cc3839acf8a7.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=2$ and $x=4.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(3)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+1)-f(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
