    "q-344354": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344354",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "In a physics experiment, the net charge on a sensor (in $mC$) after $t$ seconds is given by the function $Q(t).$ The values of $Q(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.0$ | $1.5$ | $2.0$ | $2.5$ | $3.0$ | $3.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$Q(t)$** | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n\nApproximate the sensor’s charge instantaneous rate of change at $t=2.5$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=0.5.$\n\nThe sensor’s charge at $t=2.5$ is decreasing at approximately \n{{field-1}} $mC$ per second.",
      "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{Q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{Q(t+h)-Q(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat time $t=2.5$ with step size $h=0.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(2.5) & ≈\\frac{Q(2.5+0.5)-Q(2.5-0.5)}{2(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q(3.0)-Q(2.0)}{1.0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2}{1.0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{1.0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sensor’s charge at $t=2.5$ is decreasing at approximately $3\\,mC$ per second.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
